It depends on the language, but the idea is: break exits the switch so execution doesn’t “fall through” to the next case.

Common C/Java/JavaScript/C#/PHP style
switch (x) {
  case 1:
    doSomething();
    break;            // exits the switch
  case 2:
    doOther();
    break;
  default:
    handleDefault();  // no break needed if last
}

- Multiple labels sharing one block:
case 2:
case 3:
  doGroup();
  break;

- Intentional fall-through (use carefully):
case 1:
  prepare();
  // fall through to case 2
case 2:
  finish();
  break;

Language-specific notes
- Java (modern switch, Java 14+): arrow form doesn’t need break.
int result = switch (x) {
  case 1 -> 10;
  case 2, 3 -> 20;
  default -> {
    doSomething();
    yield 0;
  }
};
- C#: classic switch needs break/return; switch expressions don’t.
return x switch {
  1 => "one",
  2 or 3 => "two or three",
  _ => "other"
};
- JavaScript: same as C; omit break to intentionally fall through.
- Go: cases break automatically; use fallthrough to continue.
switch x {
case 1:
  fmt.Println("one")       // ends here automatically
case 2:
  fmt.Println("two")
  fallthrough
case 3:
  fmt.Println("three")
default:
  fmt.Println("other")
}
- Swift: no implicit fall-through; break is optional but can be used to “do nothing”.
switch x {
case 1:
  print("one")
case 2:
  break        // match 2 and do nothing
default:
  print("other")
}
- Kotlin: when replaces switch; no break needed.
when (x) {
  1 -> println("one")
  2, 3 -> println("two or three")
  else -> println("other")
}

